Context. The previous page opens a general discussion of approximation and interpolation. Hilbert separates (1) the class of approximating functions (integral rational functions, integral algebraic functions of a field, or the first n members of any prescribed infinite system) from (2) the principle of approximation (matching Taylor coefficients, i.e. Padé-type, versus least squares). He links least squares to writing a positive quadratic form as a sum of squares, ∫Θ²(a0+…+am x^m)² dx = φ0²(a0)+φ1²(a0,a1)+…, which is Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization.

(A) Top formula. He minimizes over a0..am, b0..bm the quantity
  J = ∫_{-1}^{1} { y(x) − (a0+a1x+…+am x^m) − √(x²−1)·(b0+b1x+…+bm x^m) }² dx.
This is least-squares approximation of y by elements A(x)+√(x²−1)B(x). These are 'integral algebraic functions' of the quadratic field C(x, √(x²−1)), as announced on the previous page. Since J is quadratic in the 2m+2 unknowns, the minimizer solves the normal equations G c = r. Here G is the Gram matrix of {x^k, x^k√(x²−1)} and r holds the moments of y. Equivalently, one orthonormalizes this system and takes Fourier coefficients. (The normal-equation step is my addition; it is not written on the page.)

(B) Variants of the basis or constraints. Instead of 1, x, x², … one may take:
- any other function sequence, e.g. 1, x², x⁴, …;
- the powers with x³ omitted;
- the trigonometric system with sin x omitted;
- the approximants subject to a linear integral condition ∫_{-1}^{1} f dx = 0;
- symmetric curves, curves through a fixed point, or curves with a fixed tangent.
His claim: convergence 'probably' takes place just the same, except that the resulting infinite series no longer represents the approximated function. In modern terms, the least-squares approximants converge in L² to the orthogonal projection of y onto the closed span of the admissible set. That projection is y itself only when the admissible set is L²-dense.

(C) Literature cited:
- Stieltjes, separate offprint 'Über Kettenbrüche' = Recherches sur les fractions continues, Ann. Toulouse 1894–95;
- C. Jordan, Cours d'analyse, vol. 2, p. 257;
- A. A. Markov, Acta Math. 19, p. 93 (very likely 'Deux démonstrations de la convergence de certaines fractions continues', 1895).
These references date the page to about 1895 or later.

(D) Theorem, continued on the next page. If f is a polynomial ('rationale Function', meaning ganze rationale) of degree m with ∫_{-1}^{1} f² dx = 1, then max_{[-1,1]} |f| stays below a bound depending only on the degree. Proof 'with Kugelfunctionen' (my completion):
1. Write f = Σ_{k=0}^m c_k p_k with p_k = √((2k+1)/2)·P_k orthonormal, so Σ c_k² = 1.
2. Since |P_k(x)| ≤ 1 on [-1,1], Cauchy–Schwarz gives |f(x)| ≤ Σ|c_k|√((2k+1)/2) ≤ (Σ_{k=0}^m (2k+1)/2)^{1/2} = (m+1)/√2.
3. This is sharp: equality holds for f(x) = K_m(x,1)/√K_m(1,1) at x = 1, where K_m is the Christoffel–Darboux kernel.
